Native runtime tracing test (trace/)
====================================

The 'trace' test exercises the native HAProxy trace source 'otel' while the
filter serves traffic.  It is the only scenario that starts the source itself:
run-trace.sh passes '-dt otel:state:clean' to the instance, so the records land
in the log beside the instance output.  The exporters of this scenario write to
/dev/null, which makes the test independent of a collector.

The configuration drives ordinary traffic through the frontend and produces one
error per response: a scope parents its span under 'orphan span', a span that
the server-unavailable scope alone creates and that never appears while the
backend is alive.  Span creation therefore fails, and the filter reports the
failure through the trace source and through its error counters.


Files
-----

  trace/otel.cfg    OTel filter configuration (the traced scopes)
  trace/haproxy.cfg HAProxy frontend/backend configuration
  trace/otel.yml    Exporter, processor, reader and provider definitions


Records
-------

The source reports at the user and state levels.  A run over the driven traffic
leaves these records in the log:

  attach   filter attached
  detach   filter detaching
  event    dispatching event, event processing completed or failed
  scope    running scope, scope processing completed
  scope    scope skipped, ACL mismatch (the scope of the /other path)
  span     allocated runtime span, processing scope span, span started
  span     finishing marked span, span processing completed
  span     releasing finished runtime span
  context  context extracted, destroying marked context, releasing finished context
  action   processing instrument creation, instrument created
  action   processing instrument update, instrument recording completed
  action   processing log record, log record emitted
  action   processing context extraction
  scope    processing set-var actions, processing unset-var action
  error    span parent resolution failed, span lookup or allocation failed,
           scope processing failed, soft runtime error

Other records need a running instance and its admin CLI:

  % echo "flt-otel rate 0"                   | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/haproxy.sock
  % echo "flt-otel disable @otel-test-trace" | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/haproxy.sock
  % echo "flt-otel hard-errors"              | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/haproxy.sock

The first two make the next stream skip attachment, the record naming the rate
limit or the globally disabled filter.  The third switches the soft error of
the response scope to its hard form, logged at the error level with the filter
disabled for that stream; the detach that follows closes the span and the
context left open, adding 'finishing span on detach' and 'destroying context on
detach' to the log.  Restore the instance afterwards with 'flt-otel rate 100',
'flt-otel enable @otel-test-trace' and 'flt-otel soft-errors'.


Running the test
----------------

The run-trace.sh script runs this configuration.  From the test/ directory:

  % ./run-trace.sh

Then drive a few requests and read the records:

  % curl -sS http://127.0.0.1:10080/ >/dev/null
  % curl -sS http://127.0.0.1:10080/other >/dev/null
  % grep '|otel|' _logs/_log-run-trace-*

The runner takes the same optional arguments as the other runners, plus a fourth
that replaces the trace specification:

  % ./run-trace.sh ../../haproxy/haproxy haproxy.pid "" otel:user:clean

The trace source itself is controlled through the HAProxy CLI.  To keep only
failures, clear the selected events and enable the error event:

  % echo "trace otel event none"  | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/haproxy.sock
  % echo "trace otel event error" | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/haproxy.sock

A trace can also follow one filter configuration or one scope; 'trace otel lock
filter' and 'trace otel lock scope' take the identifiers used in otel.cfg.  The
source needs a build with the native trace subsystem, which HAProxy enables by
default and a USE_TRACE=0 build drops.  Section 8 of README describes the source
and its levels.
